config ESP32_SYS_TIMER
	bool "ESP32 sys-timer support (ESP32Cx series)"
	depends on SOC_SERIES_ESP32C2 || SOC_SERIES_ESP32C3 || \
		(SOC_SERIES_ESP32C5 && !SOC_ESP32C5_LPCORE) || SOC_SERIES_ESP32C61 || \
		(SOC_SERIES_ESP32C6 && !SOC_ESP32C6_LPCORE) || SOC_SERIES_ESP32H2 || \
		(SOC_SERIES_ESP32P4 && !SOC_ESP32P4_LPCORE)
	default y
	select TICKLESS_CAPABLE
	select TIMER_HAS_64BIT_CYCLE_COUNTER
	select SYSTEM_TIMER_HAS_DISABLE_SUPPORT if MCUBOOT
	help
	  This option enables the system timer driver for the Espressif ESP32Cx
	  and provides the standard "system clock driver" interface.

	  ESP32-S3 under CONFIG_SMP has a real, independently-confirmed
	  correctness bug in the default CCOUNT-based XTENSA_TIMER (CCOUNT is a
	  private per-core register with no hardware link between the two
	  cores, which corrupts cross-core tick accounting), and this driver's
	  genuinely-shared SYSTIMER counter is the architecturally correct fix
	  -- the same hardware timer ESP-IDF itself uses on this chip. It is
	  deliberately NOT yet wired up as an ESP32-S3 option here: with it
	  enabled, esp_wifi_init() hangs during PHY/RF bring-up, for reasons
	  not yet root-caused (confirmed independent of CPU count and of the
	  generic esp_timer_get_time() path -- the hang is inside the wifi
	  blob's own init sequence). drivers/timer/esp32_sys_timer.c's
	  smp_timer_init() (the per-core-alarm support this would need) is in
	  place and does not affect any existing single-core user of this
	  driver, so enabling this for ESP32-S3 later is a Kconfig-only change
	  once that hang is understood.
